NAME
    cat - print files, or copy the console to the output

SYNOPSIS
    cat [file | glob | -] ...

DESCRIPTION
    Prints each file named in turn, byte for byte: nothing is added or
    dropped. A glob (* or ? in the last component, case folded) expands
    to the matching files of that directory; "-" is the console, read
    without echo up to Ctrl-D. With no argument cat copies the console
    to the output until Ctrl-D. cat is /BIN/CAT.

    Every name is checked before anything is printed: one that is not
    found (or a glob that matches nothing) stops cat with
    "cat: not found: NAME".

    The shell also has a built-in cat, used only when /BIN/CAT is
    missing; it prints one file, with no globs and no "-". `type` is
    always that built-in.

LIMITS
    Up to 24 names in all, after glob expansion; more are dropped.

EXAMPLES
    cat /README.TXT                print a file
    cat /FRUIT.TXT /FRUIT2.TXT     two files, one after the other
    cat /MAN/C*                    every page whose name starts with C
    cat /FRUIT.TXT - /README.TXT   a file, typed lines up to Ctrl-D,
                                   then another file

NOTES
    Removed from the P8X version: > and >> redirection and | pipes
    (Y1/OS has none yet), so cat cannot join files into a new one.
    New: several names in any mix, and "-" for the console.
    Ported from P8X os/commands/cat.c 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
    type, more, head, tail, stdin, man
